WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:03.570
In this video we present a set of
studies that led us to establish the

00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:05.463
Uncanny Valley of Haptics theory.

00:00:05.463 --> 00:00:07.529
The Uncanny Valley was first used to

00:00:07.529 --> 00:00:10.590
describe a visually triggered revulsion to
the hyper-realistic

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:14.093
avatars that were almost but not quite
perfectly human.

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:17.740
Now we show that a similar effect
can be observed for our sense of touch.

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:21.280
We generate an illusion of
spacial touch inside virtual reality

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:26.200
by providing synchronous vibrations of
different amplitudes to two handheld controllers.

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:29.760
The hands are also connected in the 
virtual environment by a wooden dowel.

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:31.470
We then show a small white marble

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:34.770
that seems to knock on the dowel at
different locations as we vary the

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:38.489
vibratory sensations. The touches are
felt to be located in the empty space

00:00:38.489 --> 00:00:42.600
between the hands. When we produce the
same stimulation without the marble the

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:45.960
apparent out-of-the-body spatial
location of the touch sensations will

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:49.980
persist. However, where improvements in
the spatialization of touch created a

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:54.090
worse subjective experience than a
generic vibration of equal amplitude to

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:58.109
both hands, the experience was improved
when the participants moved the virtual

00:00:58.109 --> 00:01:02.460
dowel up and down; thereby making them
the agent of the tactile sensations.

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:06.659
In this paper we show for the first time
that improvements in haptics might fall

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:12.200
into an uncanny valley, and we delineate key
features to avoid this Uncanny Valley of Haptics.

